    <!-- Watch Out For -->
    <section class="exam-section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff3cd 0%, #ffe5a0 100%); border-left-color: #ffc107;">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation section-icon" style="color: #856404;"></i> Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Quantity over Quality:</strong> Believing that listing many technologies is better than deep evaluation. (IB Feedback: "better responses provided a balanced account").</li>
            <li><strong>Ethics vs. Logic:</strong> Weaker responses often drift into purely ethical debate without addressing the underlying computational complexity or hardware constraints.</li>
            <li><strong>Isolation:</strong> Forgetting that the Case Study is part of the CS curriculum; students must link findings to specific syllabus content (e.g., how vSLAM uses Matrix math or ADTs).</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Big Ideas -->
    <section class="content-section" style="border-top-color: var(--accent-color);">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb section-icon"></i> Big Ideas to Consider</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Expertise and Authority:</strong> How do we weigh the "knowledge" provided by a commercial company (marketing white paper) against that of an academic researcher?</li>
                <li><strong>Predictive Knowledge:</strong> To what extent can we rely on "simulated" research to predict the success of a technology in a "messy" real-world emergency?</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

